###############################################################################
# Q11 -- Computational resources
###############################################################################

# Fine-tuning, training, MLIP MD (3 ns, 0.5 fs) and NEB used NVIDIA A100
# GPUs; AIMD ran on CPUs. No times are given.

###############################################################################
# Q12 -- Gaps and ambiguities (prose; not triples)
###############################################################################

# - Force MAEs are read from the numbers printed in the heat maps of Fig. 2a-e and Fig. 4a-c (text layer, one row per system and one column per training-set size). The MoS2 values stated in the text (0.039 eV/A for MACE fine-tuned on 200 configurations, 0.030 and 0.013 eV/A for MACE at 2,000) match the heat maps.
# - Energy MAEs, stride-10 results, universal-model (zero-shot) errors and the stride-1 MoS2 scaling are in Supplementary Notes and Fig. 3 only and are not encoded. The system abbreviations CDP, CPP, L-Pyro and PhOH are not expanded in this paper (Refs. [41, 42]).
# - MatterSim-v1.0.0-5M is the registry model MatterSim-v1; the other universal checkpoints (MACE-MP-0 small, SevenNet-0, GRACE-1L-OAM, ORB-v2) are named as base models by label. SevenNet is a NequIP implementation; MatterSim applies the M3GNet method.
# - Author affiliations are not encoded (ORCIDs are those deposited with the DOI metadata, source: datacite).
# - Evaluation roles and data, base models, inference timings with their system sizes, and the source location of each value are recorded in the encoding input (artifacts/kg/encoding/specs/hanseroth2026data.yaml) until the schema has terms for them.

